  "query_preview": "..A weak upper low is situated over Mexico and will \nslowly push to the east through Thursday before lifting to the north \nthrough New Mexico and Texas on Friday. The return flow around the \nlow will bring some additional moisture into eastern Arizona. This \nwill allow for some showers and isolated thunderstorms to develop \nacross southern Apache and Navajo counties through Thursday. While \nwe are not expecting any severe storms, there may be some stronger \nstorms with lightning and gusty outflows. \n\nThe rest of the region will continue to remain warm but will stay \ndry though we may some additional cloud cover as the mid level \nmoisture increases a bit. Temperatures will be around 5-10 degrees \nabove normal through Friday. \n\nThe low becomes centered over Mexico by Friday and then starts to \nlift northward into NM/TX. This will end the return flow and shift \nthe moisture off to our east, bringing an end to any shower activity \nacross our forecast area, though there is an outside chance of a \nshower along the AZ/NM border. Overall, expect Friday to be warm and \ndry with westerly afternoon breezes.\n\nThe models have started to key back in on a deepening Pacific trough \nas we head into the weekend and then into the new week. The position \nof the trough will be the determining factor in what kind of weather \nwe will receive but current indications are for stronger \nsouthwesterly winds and a return to near normal temperatures \narriving on Saturday and hanging around through at least the middle \nof next week. As far a precipitation goes, chances look pretty bleak \nas the trough doesn't dig far enough south to bring in the necessary \nmoisture. With conditions remaining dry and relative humidities \ndropping into the single digits across much of the region, add in \nthe increase in wind, and we may start to see some near-critical \nfire weather conditions return over the weekend.",
